        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(net-policy): preserve diagnostics after redacted URL query secrets",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where redacting sensitive query values from URL-like diagnostic text could remove the trailing error detail after the secret value.\n\nThe fallback path in `redactSensitiveUrlLikeString()` handles arbitrary text that is not parseable as one complete URL. Its query-value regex previously consumed until `&`, so text such as:\n\n```text\nfailed https://x.test/cb?token=abc timed out\n```\n\ncould be reduced to:\n\n```text\nfailed https://x.test/cb?token=***\n```\n\nThat masked the secret, but it also dropped the useful diagnostic suffix: `timed out`.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis change keeps the redaction behavior narrow: sensitive query values are still replaced with `***`, but fallback matching stops before surrounding prose, whitespace, or common delimiters.\n\nAfter reviewer feedback, the fallback also consumes quoted query values before preserving the surrounding prose, so `?token=\"abc\"` becomes `?token=\"***\"` instead of leaving the quoted value visible.\n\nThe parseable full-URL path remains unchanged. This only tightens the heuristic fallback used for URL-like substrings inside larger diagnostic messages.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators and developers keep the remediation context in logs and CLI errors while secrets are still hidden.\n\nFor example, a message like:\n\n```text\nfailed https://x.test/cb?token=abc timed out\n```\n\nnow becomes:\n\n```text\nfailed https://x.test/cb?token=*** timed out\n```\n\ninstead of losing the timeout reason.\n\n## Evidence\n\nAdded focused regression tests in `packages/net-policy/src/redact-sensitive-url.test.ts` for arbitrary diagnostic text with sensitive query parameters followed by prose, quoted values, and bracket/delimiter-wrapped diagnostics.\n\nValidation run:\n\n```powershell\n$env:CI='true'\npnpm exec vitest run --config test/vitest/vitest.unit.config.ts packages/net-policy/src/redact-sensitive-url.test.ts\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```text\nTest Files  1 passed (1)\nTests       19 passed (19)\n```\n\nReal diagnostic path proof through `resolveHttpMcpServerLaunchConfig()` in `src/agents/mcp-http.ts`, run against the PR branch with malformed HTTP MCP URLs so the production invalid-URL diagnostic path calls `redactSensitiveUrlLikeString()`:\n\n```powershell\nnode --import tsx .artifacts\\net-policy-real-call-path-proof.mjs\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nits url is not a valid URL: https://[bad-host/cb?token=*** timed out\nits url is not a valid URL: https://[bad-host/cb?token=\"***\" timed out\nits url is not a valid URL: https://[bad-host/cb?token=***), retrying\n```\n\nRelevant call chain:\n\n```text\nHTTP MCP config validation\n  -> resolveHttpMcpServerLaunchConfig(...)\n  -> invalid URL diagnostic reason\n  -> redactSensitiveUrlLikeString(...)\n  -> fallback URL-like query redaction\n  -> sensitive query value replacement\n```\n\nNon-goals:\n\n- This does not replace the fallback with a full URL lexer.\n- This does not change userinfo redaction.\n- This does not broaden which query names are considered sensitive.\n\nAI-assisted: Yes, prepared with Codex GPT-5.5 xhigh review assistance.",
